the impact of the pandemic on a mental  health of Canadians is still being  measured but we know social isolation  uncertainty about the future is having  an impact and it's especially acute in  indigenous communities  a new survey this week from stats Canada  found 60% of indigenous people say their  mental health has worsened since the  onset of physical distancing measures  overall 38 percent reported fair or poor  mental health that's 22 percent higher  than in the most recent Aboriginal  peoples survey well before Kovan 19 too  many young indigenous people were crying  out for help as Jeff simple tells us  there's at least one new resource now  offering help that's a phone call away  Ashley Cummings childhood photos carry  mixed emotions every day the good times  growing up in Nunavut snowmobiling with  her parents and the tough times as a  teenager losing several cousins and  friends to suicide suicide has been a  very prominent part of my life for well  since I was a young child as an E nuke  she knows all too well how the suicide  rate among Inuit is one of the highest  in the world almost 10 times the  Canadian average and when she was 16 she  almost became one of those statistics  cuz it was just such a very visceral  pain that I was being or I guess I was  almost subjecting myself through because  I didn't know how to deal with any of it  but she found a lifeline in the form of  a text message conversation with an  anonymous volunteer from Kids Help Phone  they were able to figure it out and help  me take the advice and listen and and  get me out of that crisis mode thank you  for calling Kids Help Phone how can I  help you  the Canadian charity has provided  counseling services for over three  decades and launched a new program last  year targeting indigenous youth looking  of course a service use and doing  everything we can to ensure we're  reaching indigenous young people where  they live and and that our services are  meeting their unique needs the manager  of the program called finding hope says  they're expanding and promoting their  services in indigenous communities such  as pine house Saskatchewan the remote  northern village is home to breathtaking  beauty and terrible poverty Kids Help  Phone is partnering with a local  photography club helping youth to see  the world through a different lens  Photography saved my life and in the  Beauty seeing the beauty through a lens  and instead of looking at the past you  know that and going through the rough  times again for  many First Nations maintain Inuit kids  across Canada they don't have access to  health or mental health services or  supports on a regular basis Kids Help  Phone offers support remotely 24/7 by  phone online chat and text message if  young people's eyes are on a screen  right now then that's where the mental  health supports need to be at the start  of the year they were receiving more  than a thousand text messages per month  from indigenous youth and that was  before the biggest mental health crisis  the organization has ever seen  go home and stay home this is what we  all need to be doing  and I just know how stressful it is for  a healthcare system for my colleagues  and for families the kovat 19 pandemic  and locked it saw demand for kids help  phone services skyrocket more than 1500  cries for help  calls and messages every day up three  hundred and fifty percent compared to  last year they're dealing with isolation  they're stressed more stress than  they've ever been before  there's a lot of grieving and loss young  people who aren't able to connect with  their friends maybe they're missing  graduation and then we've also seen a  real increase in contacts around other  things like abuse and neglect so for  some young people home is not a safe  place the charity has called emergency  services around ten times a day during  the pandemic when needed to respond to a  suicide attempt and that need is  greatest in communities that were  already struggling the anxiety that  indigenous youth feel is heightened in a  lot of respects for for a number of  reasons I mean there isn't the same  access to health care in a lot of these  remote communities we're telling people  over and over again wash your hands wash  your hands but now we have a hundred or  so First Nations that are on water  advisories right now where that might  not be safe we've got poverty which is  affecting so many indigenous young  people and we don't have space to  socially isolate or physically isolate  within the home these things are causing  anxiety for young people faced with that  unprecedented need kids help phone  called for backup asking ordinary  Canadians to volunteer to provide  emotional support to anonymous strangers  over text message the charity's chief  youth officer admits it was a big ask we  put a call out we said hey people across  Canada raise your hand join us take a  training it's a 36-hour training they do  a police background check and then they  sign on to our platform and the response  was overwhelming we had almost nine  thousand Canadians raise their hand and  say that they wanted to be a part of  supporting young people which to me was  huge we are on track right now to  train more people in the next few weeks  than we have trained in the last two  years together one of those volunteer  crisis responders says it's a win-win  there's very few volunteer opportunities  I believe that you can walk at the end  of your shift or walk at the end your  day and say wow I've potentially saved  someone's life today and thanks to those  volunteers for the first time in its  history Kids Help Phone is now also  offering services to adults more and  more adults were coming to us and they  were starting the conversation with I'm  so sorry I'm not a kid or can I use the  service and we realize there aren't  enough places for adults who are also  going through the same distress the same  isolation it's a tough time but good  things are coming from it  Dunham's says they've also received a  large number of indigenous volunteers  offering to provide support to others in  need one of them is Ashley Cummings now  a member of the charity's indigenous  Advisory Council I feel like I'm doing  so much better now it definitely makes  me emotional knowing that a lot of other  youth are facing very similar or the  same kind of circumstances where they're  it just feels like there's too much I  think my message would be to just give  yourself one more day it's all about  those little baby steps that build up to  large feats of figuring out your mental  health  [Music]  anyone who needs mental health support  can call kids help phone at one eight  hundred six six eight six eight six  eight or text six eight six eight six  eight the service is available 24 hours  a day and as we mentioned it's for kids  and adults
 

